First Author: Singh
Title: CT-Based Lung Size Matching in Delayed Chest Closure for Systemic Sclerosis Lung Transplantation
Source number: 47  ￼

Very short summary: This single-center retrospective study evaluated delayed chest closure (DCC) after bilateral lung transplantation in systemic sclerosis recipients and incorporated CT-based volumetric size matching into preoperative prediction. Five key findings were: (1) 92 systemic sclerosis recipients were included, and 32 (34.8%) underwent DCC; (2) DCC recipients had similar survival to primary chest closure (PCC) recipients at 30 days, 90 days, 1 year, and 5 years; (3) DCC recipients had more postoperative morbidity, including longer ventilation, more postoperative mechanical support, and longer ICU stay; (4) two CT-derived donor-recipient size-matching ratios were significantly associated with DCC; and (5) a preoperative multivariable logistic regression model achieved mean AUC 0.82 (95% CI 0.70–0.94) for predicting DCC occurrence. Figures 1–3 and Tables 1–3 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

